    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-wurttemberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Frederick I of Württemberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-wurttemberg">Frederick I of Württemberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seele-Friedrich_I..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johann Baptist Seele, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Württemberg was a creation of the Napoleonic reorganisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-wurttemberg">Frederick II, Duke of Württemberg</a> from 1797, was a large and forceful prince who had been raised to the dignity of Elector in 1803, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess</a> compensated German princes for lands lost west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> with secularised church territories and former imperial cities. In 1805 he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-pressburg-1805">Peace of Pressburg</a> rewarded him with further territory and recognition of full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. On 1 January 1806 he proclaimed himself King Frederick I. Joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> in July 1806, he absorbed the lands of mediatised counts and princes such as the Hohenlohe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-waldburg">Waldburg</a> and Königsegg families, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-mergentheim">Mergentheim</a> in 1809, and in 1810 the city of Ulm, received from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Within a few years the old duchy had roughly doubled in area and population. The new kingdom joined the largely Lutheran heartland on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> to Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-swabia">Upper Swabia</a>, the former imperial cities and the Hohenlohe plain, creating the territory that Württemberg kept until 1918. Frederick abandoned Napoleon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fulda">Treaty of Fulda</a> of November 1813 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> guaranteed his possessions, so that the kingdom survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> intact.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stuttgart.jpg" alt="Stuttgart" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neues_Schloss_Schlossplatzspringbrunnen_Schlossplatz_Stuttgart_2015_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Julian Herzog (Website), CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Württemberg was a land of small farms, vineyards and villages, where the custom of dividing inheritances among all heirs left many families with plots too small to live on. Rural households therefore combined farming with crafts such as weaving, woodworking and metalwork, which produced a large pool of skilled labour. Hardship drove heavy emigration, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> region after the famine of 1816–1817 and later in great numbers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Queen Catherine, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-wurttemberg">William I</a>, founded charitable associations and a savings bank during the famine, and in 1818 the king established an agricultural institute at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-hohenheim">Hohenheim</a> and the agricultural festival at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blood-court-at-cannstatt">Cannstatt</a>, the origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannstatter-wasen">Cannstatter Wasen</a>. Württemberg formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1828 and entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollverein">German Customs Union</a>, the Zollverein, in 1834. The state railways opened their first line in 1845, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-steinbeis">Ferdinand von Steinbeis</a>, who joined the government's office for trade and industry in 1848 and later headed it, promoted technical schools, exhibitions and the transfer of foreign techniques. By the end of the century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> had become a centre of engineering: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottlieb-daimler">Gottlieb Daimler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-maybach">Wilhelm Maybach</a> built petrol engines in Cannstatt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-bosch">Robert Bosch</a> opened his workshop in Stuttgart in 1886, and Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-zeppelin">Ferdinand von Zeppelin</a> flew his first rigid airship over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> in 1900. The population reached about 2.4 million by 1910.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tauberbischofsheim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tauberbischofsheim.jpg" alt="Battle of Tauberbischofsheim" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tauberbischofsheim">Battle of Tauberbischofsheim</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beer_Bischofsheim_1866.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wilhelm Amandus Beer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, Württemberg had to supply troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, and its contingent fought in the campaigns of 1806–1807 and 1809, including operations against the risings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a>. In 1812 it sent roughly 15,000 men into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, of whom only a few hundred are said to have returned. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fulda">Treaty of Fulda</a> the Württemberg army fought on the Allied side, and in 1814 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-wurttemberg">Crown Prince William</a> commanded an Allied corps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The federal fortress of Ulm, built from the 1840s, was among the largest fortifications in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In June 1849 Württemberg soldiers dispersed the rump of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt National Assembly</a>, which had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. In the war of 1866 the kingdom joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the southern German states against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and its division was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tauberbischofsheim">Tauberbischofsheim</a> in July. Afterwards it signed a secret alliance with Prussia and reorganised its army on the Prussian model. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870–1871 the Württemberg division fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worth">Wörth</a> and in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Under the military convention that accompanied entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, the Württemberg army became the XIII Army Corps of the imperial army, with its own war ministry in Stuttgart.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-wurttemberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-i-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="William I of Württemberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-wurttemberg">William I of Württemberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6nig_Wilhelm_I_W%C3%BCrtt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joseph Karl Stieler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Württemberg had a long tradition of representative estates, which King Frederick I abolished at the end of 1805 in order to rule as an absolute monarch. He centralised the administration, imposed uniform laws on the old and new territories and integrated Catholic and Protestant subjects in one state church system under royal control. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-wurttemberg">William I</a>, king from 1816 to 1864, restored representative government through the constitution of 25 September 1819, agreed between the king and an assembly of estates rather than simply granted. It created a parliament of two chambers, a Chamber of Lords for the nobility and a Chamber of Deputies that included representatives of the clergy, the nobility and the towns and districts, and guaranteed civil rights and the independence of the courts. Württemberg became one of the leading constitutional states of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with an efficient, university-trained civil service drawn largely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>. In March 1848 William appointed a liberal ministry under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-romer">Friedrich Römer</a>, though conservative government returned after 1849. King Charles, who reigned from 1864 to 1891, accepted the kingdom's entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, and his successor William II, a popular and unpretentious monarch, approved the reform of 1906 that made the lower chamber wholly elected. Within the empire Württemberg retained its own army administration, postal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> service and railways, along with taxes on beer and spirits.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tubingen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tubingen.jpg" alt="Tübingen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altstadt-tuebingen-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dktue, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Württemberg's influence was greatest in culture and technology. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tubingen">University of Tübingen</a> and its Protestant seminary trained clergy, officials and scholars, and the theologian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-christian-baur">Ferdinand Christian Baur</a> and his pupil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-friedrich-strauss">David Friedrich Strauss</a>, whose <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-life-of-jesus-critically-examined">Life of Jesus</a></em> of 1835 caused a sensation, made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> school central to modern biblical criticism. The Swabian school of poets, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-uhland">Ludwig Uhland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinus-kerner">Justinus Kerner</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-morike">Eduard Mörike</a>, gave the kingdom a literary reputation, and the novelist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-hesse">Hermann Hesse</a> was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calw">Calw</a> in 1877. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietism</a> remained a strong force in religious life, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korntal">Korntal</a>, founded in 1819, became a model Pietist community. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> was born in Ulm in 1879, though his family soon moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. In industry, the inventions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimler-motoren-gesellschaft">Daimler</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-maybach">Maybach</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-bosch-gmbh">Bosch</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> one of the birthplaces of the motor car and its electrical equipment, firms that still bear their founders' names, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-zeppelin">Zeppelin</a>'s airships, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margarete-steiff">Margarete Steiff</a>'s toy animals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giengen-an-der-brenz">Giengen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marklin">Märklin</a>'s model trains at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goppingen">Göppingen</a> became widely known. Politically, Württemberg showed how a southern German middle state could combine monarchy with a working constitution, and its liberal and democratic movements fed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt National Assembly</a> of 1848. The skilled, decentralised industry that grew from its small-farm economy remains characteristic of the modern state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war.jpg" alt="Austro-Prussian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht-bei-koeniggraetz-von-georg-bleibtreu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georg Bleibtreu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Württemberg's independence ended in stages. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866 dissolved the German Confederation and left the southern states isolated, and defeat forced Württemberg into an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Its troops fought alongside Prussia in 1870, and by the November Treaties of 1870 the kingdom joined the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, proclaimed in January 1871. It kept its monarchy, its own government and important reserved rights, but foreign policy, war and peace and most economic legislation passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and the kingdom became one federal state among many, the fourth largest by population. Industrial growth continued, while politics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> was long dominated by the ministry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-von-mittnacht">Hermann von Mittnacht</a>, from 1876 to 1900, before the Social Democrats, the Centre and the democratic People's Party gained ground. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> brought heavy losses and food shortages. When revolution spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in November 1918, a provisional government took power in Stuttgart, and King William II, who left the city on 9 November, formally renounced the throne on 30 November 1918. The kingdom became the Free People's State of Württemberg, a republic within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Germany</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> its territory was divided between the American and French zones, and in 1952 it was merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> to form the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Raised to a kingdom by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1806, Württemberg doubled in size, became one of the first constitutional states of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1819 and, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> after 1871, a centre of skilled engineering where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimler-motoren-gesellschaft">Daimler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-bosch-gmbh">Bosch</a> helped create the motor industry.</em></p>

